# DESCRIPTION

The backend turns a compute shader into the words MaliCore's dispatch runs, and a vertex or a fragment shader into the words its draw runs.
It runs on the device, when a pipeline is made, and on the host, in tests.
It is Swift, tier 0.

The work, in order: calls are inlined, the IR's passes run and scalarise, instructions are selected over virtual registers, FAU and constant sources are fitted to the instruction limits, registers are allocated, message slots and waits are placed, `reconverge` and `end` are added, the branches are resolved, and the ISA validator checks the result (a broken rule is `invalidOutput`).
The output `ComputeShader` holds the code, the register class (32 when every register is in r0 to r15 or r48 to r63), the preload mask, the workgroup size, the stack and workgroup memory bytes, the FAU words, whether workgroup merging is allowed and the flush mode.

## The ABI

Preloads are read in place: global invocation id in r60 to r62 (mask bits 12 to 14), workgroup id in r57 to r59, local invocation id in r55 and r56 (unpacked at the start).
The lane id is the special FAU `lane_id`; a shader that reads it, uses workgroup memory or a barrier does not allow merging.
The dispatch base is not applied: the run state's offset is zero.
Workgroup memory is `workgroup_local_pointer` plus a byte offset; stack, private and function variables and spills are the thread's stack, reached at `thread_local_pointer` with access `force`.

## Vertex and fragment shaders

`compileGraphics` compiles the first vertex or fragment entry point (or the one named) the same way, into the data MaliCore builds a draw from: a `VertexShader` or a `FragmentShader` (code, register class 64, preload mask, `GraphicsFauLayout`, and the locations the shader reads and writes).
The two stages and the driver agree on the ABI below, all of it in `GraphicsShader.swift`.

A vertex shader is one program for both IDVS passes, and it runs whole in each: it ends in two groups of stores, each behind a test of `shader_output`: the position (bit 0) and the generic varyings (bit 2).
Preloads are r59 (the vertex's slot in the hardware vertex buffer, always), r60 (the vertex index) and r61 (the instance index); an attribute (a Location input) is read with `LD_ATTR_IMM` from table 0 at the index of its location, taking r60 and r61.
The position is divided by w and put through the viewport transform in the shader (valhall 14.3.3): `r` is the reciprocal of w clamped to a magnitude of 2^15, `x * r * scale + offset` for x, y and z, and r is the stored w; the scale and offset are FAU words.
The record is stored with `STORE.i128.istream` at the address `LEA_BUF_IMM` gives from table 61, buffer 0, and r59.
A varying is stored with `STORE.estream` through table 61, buffer 1, at 16 bytes times its location, so the fragment shader finds it knowing nothing of the vertex shader, and `varyingBytes` is the size to give the draw.
A varying the fragment shader declares NoPerspective is stored times the position's w, chosen at compile time by `BackendOptions.nonPerspectiveMask` (the fragment shader's `nonPerspectiveMask`), or at run time by a bit of a FAU word when the option is nil.
PointSize is accepted and not stored, so the position record keeps its basic format.

A fragment shader's preloads are r60 (the coverage, always), r59 (the pixel position: FragCoord's x and y are those plus a half), r58 (the facing) and r61 (centroid inputs).
A Location input is read with `LD_VAR_BUF_IMM` at 16 bytes times its location (flat for an integer or Flat input), once, at the start; FragCoord's z and w and a NoPerspective input's multiplier are `LD_VAR_SPECIAL`.
Interpolating loads wait for the one before them (the hidden register, 12c.5.5).
OpKill is `DISCARD`, and the path goes to the end.
Every path ends in the epilogue of 12c.6.4: a wait, `ATEST` (the alpha of colour 0, or 1), a wait, and one `BLEND` for each colour output, to the render target of its location and the special blend descriptor of the same number; the last carries `end`.

The vertex shader's FAU block is the viewport (three slots: scale and offset of x, then y, then z), then the non-perspective mask when read, then the push constants; the fragment shader's is the push constants.
`GraphicsFauLayout.block` builds it.
A buffer bound by descriptor is refused in these stages: the binding is the root pointer (a pointer in the push constants, read as a global pointer).

## Textures and samplers

The shader names images and samplers by (set, binding) and an element index, and the descriptor model of the driver is one bindless heap: a table of 32-byte descriptors, textures and samplers by index, which is table 1 of the resource table (table 0 is the driver's).
For each binding a fragment shader names, the driver chooses the heap index of element 0 and writes its handle (`table << 24 | index`, `DescriptorHeap.handle(index:)`) in a FAU word.
The words are the first of the fragment block, in ascending (set, binding) order (`GraphicsFauLayout.imageBindings`), and a word for the dummy sampler follows them when a fetch names no sampler (`dummySamplerWord`); the push constants follow.
`GraphicsFauLayout.block(images:dummySampler:)` fills them.

A texture access is one message.
`TEX_SINGLE` serves a sample, with the level mode `computed` (an implicit level, from the quad's derivatives), `computed_bias` (the level word is the bias), `explicit` (the level word) or `none` (an explicit level of zero, no word), and `TEX_FETCH` a fetch.
The handle source is the wide form, a register pair: word 0 the sampler's handle, word 1 the texture's, each the binding's FAU word plus the element index, computed in the shader, so an array of textures indexed with a value that differs from lane to lane costs one add.
A fetch of a plain image (OpImage of a sampled image drops the sampler) names the dummy sampler.
The staging block is one register for each operand in the order of valhall 12b.5: the coordinates, the layer, the offset word, the level word.
The wide form has no narrow word, so a constant offset, the array index and a fetch's level (zero needs none) are staged.
The result block holds the channels the shader reads, in order, 32 bits each, and its register type field (`f`, `u` or `s`) is the texel's kind.
`skip` is clear, since an implicit level needs the helper lanes' coordinates, and the shader has no `discard` flow, so helper lanes stay alive to its end; OpDemoteToHelperInvocation is a `DISCARD` that flags the lane and goes on.
The wait before the result's first reader is on the instruction before it.
A vertex shader may not sample.

## The FAU layout

`FauLayout` says where the block holds what the shader reads, in this order:
one slot for the 64-bit address of each storage or uniform buffer the shader names, in ascending (set, binding) order;
the number of workgroups (x, y, z, pad), when read;
the byte size of each buffer whose run-time array length is read, one 32-bit word each;
the push constants, word n at byte offset 4n, up to the highest word read.
`FauLayout` is plain data in MaliCore, so a dispatch needs no compiler to use it.
`FauLayout.block` builds the 64-bit words from the buffers' addresses and sizes, the push constants and the grid.
A shader that needs more than 128 slots is refused.

## Selection

i32 and f32 are one register, bool is 0 or all ones, i64 and global pointers are an even pair.
Buffer, workgroup and stack pointers are followed at compile time: the constant offset goes in the access's 16-bit field, and a run-time offset (with a power-of-two multiply folded in) is one `SHADD`.
A compare used once by a select in its block is one `CSEL`.
`fdiv`, `fsqrt`, `fexp2`, `flog2`, `fsin` and `fcos` use the sequences of valhall 11c.13: `fsqrt` is `frcp` of `frsq`, exp2 and log2 within the spec's bounds, sine and cosine within 2^-15.7 for arguments below about 411000.
Blocks end in the branches of 13.4; a block before a join carries `reconverge`; a block drains its message slots before its exits, so every block starts with none busy.

## Registers

Linear scan over one interval for each virtual register, in class 32 first and then class 64.
A shader that does not fit class 64 spills: each write of the chosen value stores, each read fills, at 8-byte slots after the shader's own stack objects, and `stackBytes` covers them.

