# BUGS

Vertex and fragment shaders have no thread storage in the draw, so a spill or a stack variable gives a non-zero `stackBytes` the driver must honour; both run in every IDVS pass entirely, so a varying's computation is also done in the position pass; derivatives other than an implicit level, FragDepth and integer colour outputs, point size and layer outputs are refused or not stored; the attribute loads and the varying and special loads are checked by the validator and, for the vertex stage's stores, on the interpreter (whose model of the hardware vertex buffer is a test's), not yet on a GPU except the position record.
Integer division and remainder, 8 and 16-bit integers, half and double floats, 64-bit atomics, cube, depth, storage and multisampled images, the gather, projection and gradient forms of sampling, texel offsets that are not constant, pointers in phis or selects and push constants read at a run-time offset are refused (`unsupported`).
Sine and cosine are wrong past about 411000 radians.
A block's slots are drained at its end, so a store before a join costs a wait there even where the join's code would not need it.
A wait in an instruction's flow field takes effect after that instruction, so the backend puts a wait on the instruction before its consumer (or on a NOP when that one issues a message or already carries a flow value), never on the consumer itself; the interpreter faults on a read the carrier's own wait would have covered.
The last-use marks skip registers a message still reads.
A texture access has not been run on the interpreter, which has no texture unit: its selection is checked against the encodings, the staging and wait rules and the validator, and on the board by gpu-test's textured quad; the wide form of the handle, the flag in the ISA's register type field and the helper lanes' retiring are the board's to confirm.
The heap handles are computed by the shader with 32-bit adds, so an index beyond the heap's size is the hardware's to refuse, and a combined image sampler is refused.
